  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Biswakarma Pooja vs The State Of Telangana on 3 March, 2022 Author: P.Naveen Rao Bench: P Naveen Rao, G.Radha Rani The Hon'Ble Sri Justice P. Naveen Rao And The Hon'Ble Smt Dr. Justice G. Radha Rani Writ Petition No. 24185 Of 2021 Date: 03.03.2022",
    "facts": "This writ petition is filed challenging the detention order dated 26.06.2021 made under Section 3(2) of the Telangana Prevention of Dangerous Activities of Bootleggers, Dacoits, Drug-Offenders, Goondas, Immoral Traffic Offenders, Land Grabbers, Spurious Seed Offenders, Insecticide Offenders, Fertiliser Offenders, Food Adulteration Offenders, Fake Document Offenders, Scheduled Commodities Offenders, Forest Offenders, Gaming Offenders, Sexual Offenders, Explosive Substances Offenders, Arms Offenders, Cyber Crime Offenders and White Collar or Financial Offenders Act, 1986 (Act 1 of 1986) as confirmed by the Government vide G.O.Rt.No.2058 dated 09.09.2021. 4. The detaining authority relied on Crime No.24 of 2021 in Sanjeevareddy Nagar Police Station, Crime No.12 of 2021 in Ramgopalpet Police Station, Crime No.10 of 2021 in Mirchowk Police Station, Crime No.5 of 2021 in Market Police Station, Crime Nos.15 and 16 of 2021 in Gopalapuram Police Station, registered against the detenu under Section 382 of Indian Penal Code (IPC) to resort to preventive detention. All the six crimes were related to snatching of mobile phones.",
    "arguments": "5. Learned counsel for the petitioner placed reliance on earlier decisions of this Court in W.P.Nos.19296 and 17307 of 2020, W.P.No.28524 of 2019 and W.P.No.16599 of 2019, wherein this Court has held that resorting to preventive detention on routine law and order offences like snatching of mobile phones is not warranted and amounts to arbitrary exercise of power and authority. 6. Learned Government Pleader fairly submits that the reasons assigned for detention are identical to the reasons assigned in the earlier detention orders which were subject matter of challenge in those writ petitions.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. Learned counsel for the petitioner placed reliance on earlier decisions of this Court in W.P.Nos.19296 and 17307 of 2020, W.P.No.28524 of 2019 and W.P.No.16599 of 2019, wherein this Court has held that resorting to preventive detention on routine law and order offences like snatching of mobile phones is not warranted and amounts to arbitrary exercise of power and authority. 6. Learned Government Pleader fairly submits that the reasons assigned for detention are identical to the reasons assigned in the earlier detention orders which were subject matter of challenge in those writ petitions.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
